{"kind":"assert","text":"Recording the custody-clobber defect durably. It was reported by ox-alpha on hugh's behalf in .tmp/NOTE-FOR-PLANNER-custody-clobber.md, because MCP delivery was defeated by the very defect being reported. A file in a gitignored directory is not a durable record, so here it is.\n\nWHAT HAPPENS. Two new actors, ox-builder and ox-checker, were minted today. Both gs actor-add runs succeeded: keys written, roster statements appended and ratified, custody saved. Seconds later both new MCP adapters failed with \"cannot act in the workroom ... unknown actor\". The local config listed only the five pre-existing actors.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, VERIFIED IN THE CODE RATHER THAN TAKEN FROM THE REPORT. internal/app/app.go rememberVerifiedFrontier sets w.Config.VerifiedFrontier and then calls w.save(), which is apphost.SaveConfig(w.MetaDir, w.Config) at app.go:448. SaveConfig marshals the whole Config and replaces the file. So persisting one field writes back every field, including the Actors map as that process last read it. Any long-lived process that verifies a read -- the resident, or an MCP adapter opened days ago -- rewrites its stale actor map over newer custody.\n\nThe file write is atomic in the sense that no reader sees half a file. It is not atomic in the sense that matters here: it is a lost update. Two writers each read, each modify a different field, and the last one to write erases the other's change. Calling the operation atomic is what made this easy to miss.\n\nWHAT IS AND IS NOT AT RISK. Roster membership for both ox actors is durable and ratified; that was never in danger. Key files were never touched. What flaps is the local custody map, which is what an adapter needs to sign. The observed evidence is severe: custody was lost and repaired twice within ten minutes, eight consecutive adapter attaches failed, and repair-immediately-before-attach lost fifteen consecutive sub-second races while the log grew from depth 8735 to 8757 under other agents' appends. Every append or verified read rolls the dice again, so single-shot local repair cannot win against a busy log.\n\nWHY THIS IS WORTH A DURABLE RECORD BEYOND ITS FIX. A new actor cannot reliably join this workroom. That is a bootstrap defect, and the two actors it locked out are the ones who found it."}